WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:09.900
Biological databases have been invaluable
for managing these data and for making them

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.309
accessible.

00:00:11.309 --> 00:00:16.099
Depending on the data that they contain, the
databases fulfil different functions.

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:20.900
However, one of the major problem is that
each of these database resources contains

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.300
a different subset of biological knowledge.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.750
Although each database can answer questions
in its domain, it cannot help with questions

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.379
that span domain boundaries.

00:00:31.379 --> 00:00:36.610
For example, the FlyBase database does not
know about orthology relationships, The Institute

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:42.289
for Genomic Research (TIGR) database can help
with orthology but not with map position and

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:46.120
the UCSC database knows nothing about the
fly.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:50.839
As this example shows, researchers must become
adept at 'database surfing' to answer many

00:00:50.839 --> 00:00:52.679
reasonable questions.

00:00:52.679 --> 00:00:58.239
Imagine, life would be much simpler if there
was a single biological database.

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:00.659
But this would be a poor solution.

00:01:00.659 --> 00:01:05.030
The diverse databases reflect the expertise
and interests of the groups that maintain

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.130
them.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:10.640
A single database would reflect a series of
compromises that would ultimately impoverish

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.090
the information resources that are available
to the scientific community.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:19.549
A better solution would be to maintain the
scientific and political independence of the

00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:23.920
databases, but allow the information that
they contain to be easily integrated to enable

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.479
cross-database queries.

00:01:26.479 --> 00:01:29.410
There are many integration challenges.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:33.400
One of the most difficult is the one that
might seem the most minor.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:39.350
How do you assign and maintain the correct
names of biological objects across databases?

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:45.500
For example, consider the DNA-damage checkpoint-pathway
gene that is named Rad24 in Saccharomyces

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.280
cerevisiae or budding yeast.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:53.310
Saccharomyces pombe or fission yeast also
has a gene named rad24 that is involved in

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.090
the checkpoint pathway.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:02:00.149
But, it is not the orthologue of the S. cerevisiae
Rad24.

00:02:00.149 --> 00:02:06.689
Instead, the correct S. pombe orthologue is
rad17, which is not to be confused with the

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:11.130
similarly named Rad17 gene in S. cerevisiae.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:17.000
Meanwhile, the human checkpoint-pathway genes
are sometimes named after the S. cerevisiae

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.390
orthologues, sometimes after the S. pombe
orthologues, and sometimes have independently

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.290
derived names.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:30.319
A more subtle problem is the clash of concepts
as users move from one database to another.

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:35.121
For example, the use of the term pseudogene,
genomic variant and even the concept of the

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:40.230
gene itself can mean radically different things
to different research communities.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:45.650
Some researchers treat the gene as the transcriptional
unit itself, whereas others extend this definition

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:50.720
to include up- and downstream regulatory elements,
and still others use the classical definitions

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.880
of cistron and genetic complementation.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.580
There are also technical challenges.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:02.379
The various biological databases use different
DBMS and none provide a standard way of accessing

00:03:02.379 --> 00:03:04.129
the data.

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:07.989
Even more challenging is the issue of updates.

00:03:07.989 --> 00:03:13.439
Biological databases are always changing,
so integration must be an ongoing task.

00:03:13.439 --> 00:03:18.019
There are main ways in integration of biological
databases can be achieved.

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:21.099
First, link integration.

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:25.189
Link integration has been by far the most
successful, because it lends itself to the

00:03:25.189 --> 00:03:27.769
haphazard nature of the web.

00:03:27.769 --> 00:03:32.079
In the familiar and ubiquitous version of
this technique, researchers begin their query

00:03:32.079 --> 00:03:37.310
with one data source, and then follow hypertext
links to related information in other data

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:38.310
sources.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:43.140
Second, a variation on link integration is
embodied in the popular sequence retrieval

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:49.140
system, which is a keyword indexing and search
system for biological databases.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.379
Sequence retrieval system is more sophisticated
than general web-based search tools, for example,

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:59.140
Google, because it recognizes the existence
of structured fields in source databases and

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:03.720
allows maintainers to explicitly relate a
field in one database to a differently named

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.620
field in another.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:10.060
Unfortunately, link integration is problematic
on several fronts.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:15.840
First, it is extraordinarily vulnerable to
naming clashes and ambiguities.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.989
For example, a naive researcher who tries
to navigate through the Rad24 gene confusion

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:26.970
using SRS or direct database-to-database linking
might wander into the wrong gene family.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:30.120
Second, there are update issues.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.410
An outgoing web link represents a leap of
faith that the page at the other end of the

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.840
link is still valid or if it exists at all.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:44.490
For example, if the curators of the S. cerevisiae
database withdraw or rename Rad24, they have

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:49.520
no way of informing all the databases that
point to the Rad24 page that an update is

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:50.539
required.

00:04:50.539 --> 00:04:53.419
Third, view integration.

00:04:53.419 --> 00:04:57.810
View integration leaves the information in
its source databases, but builds an environment

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:02.620
around the databases that makes them all seem
to be part of one large system.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:07.590
Despite the appeal of this approach, the system
has failed to catch on with the community.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.580
Fourth, data warehousing.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.780
Data warehousing can be broadly described
as bringing all the data under one roof in

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:16.960
a single database.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.710
The first step in data warehousing is to develop
a unified data model that can accommodate

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:26.080
all the information that is contained in the
various source databases.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.389
The next step is to develop a series of software
programs that will fetch the data from the

00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:35.110
source databases, transform them to match
the unified data model, and then load them

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:37.069
into the warehouse.

00:05:37.069 --> 00:05:41.380
The warehouse can then be used as a 'one-stop
shop' for answering any of the questions that

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:46.009
the source databases can handle, as well as
those that require integrated knowledge that

00:05:46.009 --> 00:05:48.669
the individual sources do not have.

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:53.819
However, it is more difficult to create a
data warehouse than it might sound.

00:05:53.819 --> 00:05:58.100
The single biggest issue is keeping the data
warehouse up to date.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:02.350
New information is being continually added
to the source databases, which means that

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:06.699
the new data must be re-imported into the
warehouse in a timely fashion or the warehouse

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:08.880
will go out of date.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.289
To make matters worse, database designs do
not stand still.

00:06:13.289 --> 00:06:17.789
Their maintainers are continually tinkering
with the data model by adding new data types,

00:06:17.789 --> 00:06:23.259
changing fields and nomenclature, and changing
the relationships among data types.

00:06:23.259 --> 00:06:27.479
This constant churn means that dump, transform
and load software that have been written for

00:06:27.479 --> 00:06:31.910
one version of a database will not necessarily
work with a later version.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.729
Fifth, web services.

00:06:34.729 --> 00:06:39.910
An important variant of link-level integration
is the concept of the web service.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:44.280
In this view, the heterogeneous collection
of loosely-linked data resources on the web

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:48.590
is turned on its head, and becomes a world
of services that are linked by service names

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:50.560
and definitions.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.100
In such a world, GenBank is no longer a database
from which users retrieve sequence entries,

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:01.889
instead it is a service that transforms sequence
accession numbers into GenBank FLAT FILES.

00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:06.729
This concept might seem a minor point, but
it allows users to establish a common framework

00:07:06.729 --> 00:07:11.600
that encompasses, for example, the BLAST search
engine, which transforms a search sequence

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.289
into a series of similarity hit records on
a selected query database.

00:07:16.289 --> 00:07:20.530
Finally, establishment of globally unique
identifiers.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:24.879
Recall the issues that the same biological
object might have multiple names, and the

00:07:24.879 --> 00:07:28.240
same name might denote multiple objects.

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:32.280
One approach is to designate an authoritative
names commission to manage the definitive

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.040
list of such names.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:39.340
Suh effort is currently being handle by the
HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee, or HGNC

00:07:39.340 --> 00:07:40.280
in short.

